00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.330
is reducing risk and cost.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.690
This is a tough challenge,
but it's not impossible.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.690
Containerization can help
your developers and operators

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.160
be more productive.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.620
And Kubernetes
coupled with Anthos

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.300
can help you run
efficiently wherever

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.258
you need to be running.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.698
So as you probably
know, containers

00:03:02.698 --> 00:03:03.990
are a necessary building block.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:07.470
That's why we're all here in
San Diego this week for sure.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.980
But they're really important
to make your teams more agile.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.020
In a nutshell,
containers are easier

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.720
to manage than traditional
solutions, be it bare metal

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:16.442
or VM based.

00:03:16.442 --> 00:03:18.900
For example, when you need to
make a change to a production

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:20.940
application, there's
no need to patch

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.610
a binary on a VM or a machine.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:26.850
Just toss out the old container
and replace it with a new one.

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.350
When operating
containers, there's

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:33.010
less infrastructure to manage,
patch, and otherwise maintain.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:35.010
Another major benefit is
the containers give you

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.380
reproducible and
consistent artifacts

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:40.830
throughout your dev, test, and
production deployment stages.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:45.030
This makes it far easier and
faster to develop and debug

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.590
your workloads.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.140
The net result is that
developers and operators

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.260
can focus more on the
development instead of managing

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.120
and patching infrastructure.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.700
But you may be thinking
to yourself containers

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.160
by themselves are not enough.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.590
You need tooling to manage
and run these container based

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:03.730
applications.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.800
Kubernetes is that tooling.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.570
Now, one of the key
features of Kubernetes

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:09.820
is that it provides an
abstraction from the underlying

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:11.050
infrastructure.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.630
This is particularly useful,
given the challenges and goals

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.760
we've discussed up till now.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.120
With Kubernetes, your
developers and service operators

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:22.510
can learn a common set of
tooling, APIs, et cetera,

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:24.490
and apply them anywhere
Kubernetes runs.

00:04:27.123 --> 00:04:29.290
Now that we know that
containers and Kubernetes help

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:33.370
us run workflows anywhere,
why would we need to?

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:35.950
Here are some of the top hybrid
and multi-cloud use cases

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:38.120
you've told us about.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:39.620
The first one on
the top left here

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.810
is invoking legacy dependencies.

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.970
While you can move certain
workloads to cloud,

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.460
there will always be some on
premises services that either

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:50.130
have not or cannot
be moved to cloud.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:52.943
In the middle on the top
is service availability.

00:04:52.943 --> 00:04:54.360
We hear all the
time that in order

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.990
to achieve your particular
latency and availability

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.420
requirements, you need to
deploy your application

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.830
across multiple regions
and serve traffic to them.

00:05:05.448 --> 00:05:06.990
Another one we hear
a lot, especially

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:10.020
in the multi-cloud context,
is developer choice.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:13.980
It's important to give your
teams access to the preferred

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.130
manage services, which
will reduce their friction

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:20.847
and help them move
a lot more quickly.

00:05:20.847 --> 00:05:22.930
And that brings us to data
locality and regulation

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:25.480
on the bottom left there.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:27.695
Regulatory requirements
often come up

00:05:27.695 --> 00:05:29.320
where you're required
to keep your data

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:33.010
and compute in specific
regions throughout the world.

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:34.540
This can be on premises.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:36.280
It could be in multiple clouds.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:38.640
It could be anywhere.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.182
Another one that's
kind of emerging--

00:05:40.182 --> 00:05:42.307
in the bottom of the last
two are sort of emerging.

00:05:42.307 --> 00:05:43.810
But local execution
comes up a lot,

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:46.530
where is providing mission
critical applications

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.070
to local, often on
premises environments.

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:53.130
The examples we have here,
warehouses, branches,

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:54.348
even factories.

00:05:54.348 --> 00:05:55.890
And you want to do
this in a way that

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:58.140
allows you to build
manage and maintain

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:03.660
these applications using modern
tooling driven from cloud.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:06.270
And finally, in the bottom
right we've got edge computing.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:07.860
It's very similar
to local execution,

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:08.943
but it's more constrained.

00:06:08.943 --> 00:06:11.640
You've got more remote
connectivity problems.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.080
And you've got non-traditional
compute environments.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.870
So we saw how adopting
containers and Kubernetes

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.965
help improve velocity,
increase portability.

00:06:21.965 --> 00:06:23.340
We saw many reasons
why you would

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.840
want to run workloads anywhere.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:26.340
But now.

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:28.770
How do you do this
efficiently and effectively

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.858
across all of these
different environments?

00:06:30.858 --> 00:06:32.150
And that's why we built Anthos.

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:36.210
It's a software stack that uses
Kubernetes as its foundation

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:39.100
and provides through additional
layers, which Amr will show us

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:41.100
in a minute,
consistency, security,

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.190
and management capabilities,
which in turn accelerates

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:46.227
your developer velocity.

00:06:46.227 --> 00:06:48.310
To tell you more about
those and how it does this,

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:49.850
I'll now turn it over to Amr.

