00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:12.330
I'll say OK, how can
I have this visibility

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.880
and centralized visibility
across all of these clusters

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:16.810
and manage my applications?

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:19.020
So OK, I got my
clusters up running.

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:20.628
I got my applications
up running.

00:09:20.628 --> 00:09:21.420
I can see them all.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:22.530
What do I do next?

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:24.780
You might want to make sure
that all of these clusters

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:27.780
are configured correctly
and to same standards

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:28.740
you have in enterprise.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.350
And this is where Anthos
conflict management comes in--

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:33.440
making sure that all your
clusters are consistent,

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:35.190
making sure that all
the configuration are

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:37.740
consistent across
any cloud provider.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:39.420
You've got your
clusters up and running.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.087
You've got your
applications up running.

00:09:41.087 --> 00:09:41.850
Everything is OK.

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:45.300
Now I want to get this
second level of visibility

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.130
into how my
applications are run.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:50.760
I want to get this deep dive
into my SLO, my performance.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:53.310
And this is where Anthos
Service Mesh comes in.

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:56.010
Anthos Service Mesh adds this
additional layer of visibility,

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:58.890
add this additional layer of
control on the infrastructure

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:01.500
layer by using Istio
to give you that

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:04.920
is almost like x-ray like
visibility across all

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.740
of your apps to see exactly
where is the leniency, which

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:10.230
apps are talking to each
other, which ones are affecting

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:11.980
the performance of
your application, which

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:15.630
is specifically critical as
you move to micro services.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:17.820
So you got your
service mesh now.

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:18.810
What do I do?

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:20.020
Let's get more application.

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:23.970
And this is where capabilities
such as Cloud Run, GCP

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:25.343
marketplace comes in.

00:10:25.343 --> 00:10:26.760
And more and more
applications are

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:31.200
going to be running from
Google and some other partners

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.750
to help you run all
of these applications.

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:35.520
As you look at
this, this actually

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:44.300
helps you look at your
application infrastructure,

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:46.730
where you are in the
journey, and get with you

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:48.950
and introduce the existing
component, whatever

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:55.050
component you need to manage
where you are in your journey.

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:57.130
Focusing a little
bit about Anthos GKE.

00:10:57.130 --> 00:11:00.480
GKE is kind of the
foundation of a lot of this.

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:06.300
GKE is our Kubernetes
distribution.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.100
It runs the first
generation of that,

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.930
as our partner mentioned,
five years ago.

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:13.470
Came in live on GCP.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:17.520
And it's one of the most widely
regarded, widely respected

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:18.840
Kubernetes platform.

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:20.520
But the idea is you
don't have to think

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:22.620
about a lot of
