00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:58.710
into the technology and the
multi-cluster observability

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:01.320
and management features.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:03.570
I'm going to start by
talking about connecting

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:07.110
your clusters to Google with
their single pane of glass.

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:08.925
And I'll then give some demos.

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:14.180
Just to start with some
of the terminology here,

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:16.190
you've got Kubernetes clusters.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:18.260
They can be on GCP.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:19.250
They can be on prem.

00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:21.080
They can be on other clouds.

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:23.870
And the first step is to
register those clusters

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:28.700
with Google's control
plane and the GKE hub.

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:31.340
Those clusters get
connected to GCP

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:33.770
by installing a connect agent.

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:36.337
And then you can log in
and interact and view

00:13:36.337 --> 00:13:37.670
the workloads in those clusters.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:44.390
We have an amazing UI for GKE.

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:48.470
And we've extended that to
Kubernetes clusters anywhere.

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:51.380
With this single
plane of glass, you

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:54.290
can interact with all your
GKE clusters, as well as

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:56.960
GKE on prem clusters, which
are actually connected

00:13:56.960 --> 00:13:58.570
when they are created.

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:00.230
Via this single
pane of glass, you

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:02.510
can monitor and
manage your workloads

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:03.740
no matter where they are.

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:06.470
You can dig into the
cluster resources

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:08.600
and actually see what the
version of the cluster

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:10.780
is, how many nodes
are in that cluster,

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:12.650
what the CPO and memory are.

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:14.330
You can actually
view and inspect

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:16.910
all the different workloads
that are running in there,

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:19.370
and whether they are
healthy, or whether they

00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:21.470
might have a problem.

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:25.040
The GKE UI also provides
additional context

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:26.930
and surfaces
relevant information.

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:29.930
For example, if there is an
error, if there is a problem

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:32.960
it'll raise those
alerts or warnings

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:35.690
and make them really
easy and actionable.

00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:38.150
And if you have
sufficient permissions,

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:42.080
you can also manipulate
resources directly from the UI.

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:44.030
For example, scaling
up a deployment

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:46.760
or changing the version
of an application.

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:48.710
So the cloud console
really provides

00:14:48.710 --> 00:14:51.350
a consistent UI
for your operators

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:55.280
and your developers across
clouds and across clusters.

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:57.720
Now I'm going to talk
about how you connect

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:01.220
your clusters to Google.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:04.460
To connect your
clusters, you run

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:07.400
a agent that runs in your
cluster and connects to Google.

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:10.520
Looking at the diagram here,
in the bottom right-hand side

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:12.380
this is your
infrastructure and this

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:14.920
is your Kubernetes clusters.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:18.650
And on the top, this is
GCP and Google Cloud.

00:15:18.650 --> 00:15:21.380
And so you can see, we've got
a few different keys and locks

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:22.010
here.

00:15:22.010 --> 00:15:24.560
These relate to different
security operations

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:25.680
that are happening.

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:28.370
I'm going to first talk
about installing the agent.

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:32.150
So you see that there's a lock
and a key with the GCP Cloud

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:33.080
logo.

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:34.940
So you install that agent.

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:39.290
And you use GCP Service
account for the authentication

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:40.810
and authorization.

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:43.400
And a encrypted connection
gets formed to Google

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:46.030
from that agent, connecting
your cluster to Google.

00:15:46.030 --> 00:15:49.190
If for any reason the
agent gets disconnected,

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:51.590
it will periodically
attempt to reconnect.

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:58.590
When you visit the cloud
console after you've

00:15:58.590 --> 00:15:59.870
connected a cluster--

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:01.800
this is in the upper
left-hand corner there.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:06.300
You can see there's a little
key and the Kubernetes icon.

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:08.820
This is actually you
logging in as you.

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:11.940
And in the bottom left is
the API server with the lock.

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:14.640
And this is where
role-based access controls

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:17.670
that are set by you and your
administrators come into play.

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:20.610
So actually, depending on
what your privileges are

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:22.650
when you log in, you
can do different things

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:25.110
from the cloud console.

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:26.790
And administrators
can essentially

00:16:26.790 --> 00:16:29.280
scope permissions
to least privilege

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:32.160
to ensure that you can only do
the things that you need to be

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:34.650
able to do in that cluster.

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:37.980
Now that I've told you
about how to securely log

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:40.295
into your clusters and
interact with them,

